cartridge "SALOON SHOOTOUT/COMPUTE GAZETTE/1986"

' SALOON SHOOTOUT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Saloon Shootout", COMPUTE!'s Gazette,
' July 1986 disk (Commodore 64 and Plus/4, machine code, files saloon.boot
' and saloon shootout; no author named on the disk). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/saloon.dis, timed in jsc64 with orig/c64run-saloon.mjs
' and orig/dump-saloon.mjs), rebuilt for the ColecoVision with a new saloon,
' cartoon townsfolk, sprites, sounds and a title tune.
'
' The game (one player): a gunslinger at the bar of a saloon, a sight
' moved with the stick, a button fires. 35 bullets, a clock of 200 ticks.
' Shoot the mugs sliding along the three shelves (10 points); a mug that
' slides off the end and smashes on the floor costs 5. Every 10 mugs a
' mouse runs along the floor: each hit sends it back the other way, faster,
' for 20, 40, 60, 80, 100 points. Now and then a shadow shows in one of the
' three balcony windows; then a figure: a masked bandit (shoot him: 25
' points; miss him and he shoots 10 of your bullets away) or a friendly
' old-timer (shooting him costs 100). Bullets come back only from the
' cards: five cards (10, J, Q, K, A) turn round a rack, three on show; shoot
' them from the ace down (WANT on the bottom line): 10 points a card, and
' the fifth refills the gun to 35. A wrong card starts the order again and
' the cards are shuffled; a card shown face down costs 10. Shooting the
' piano's keys plays a tune. 500 points once give 50 more ticks. The game
' ends when the clock or the bullets run out.
'
' From the original (orig/saloon.dis): the scene (title banner, balcony
' windows, shelves, card rack, player piano, floor, gunman at the bar);
' 35 bullets (one a shot, the button let go between shots), 200 clock
' ticks (one per 256 passes, about 0.53 s: here 32 frames), +50 ticks at
' 500 points once, a warning at 25; mugs: a spawn chance of one in two every
' other mug step on a random shelf, a step about every 15 frames, +10 a hit,
' -5 a smashed mug; the mouse after 10 mugs (hit: back the other way, faster,
' 101 - delay points: 21 41 61 81 100 here rounded to 20 40 60 80 100);
' windows: a wait of 32-63 mug steps, a shadow for 7-14 steps, then the
' figure, one in two a bandit (+25, or -10 bullets when he is not shot),
' the other -100; cards: values 1-5 shuffled into five places, three shown,
' turning every 31 frames, one random place shown face down (-10), shot
' in order from 5 down (+10), the fifth refills to 35, a wrong card restarts
' the order with a new shuffle; the piano plays one of three tunes in turn;
' the score never goes below 0; a high score; the game ends at time 0 or
' with no bullets.
'
' Liberties: a free sight sprite moved in pixels (2 a frame, 3 after a
' moment's hold) instead of a gunman sliding along the bottom row with a
' separate height marker; hits are tested against each thing's box (a
' little larger than the mug for fairness) instead of the screen cell; mugs
' slide a pixel every other frame instead of a cell every 15 frames; the
' figure stays 40 frames (the C64: about 31) so a person can react; a hit
' bandit loses his hat and a hit old-timer ducks; the shuffle redraws the
' cards at once; new piano phrases (the C64's three tunes are not used) and
' a new title tune; a title, a credits page and messages on the bottom line.
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller): the stick moves the sight,
' either button fires. On the title, up/down choose PLAY or CREDITS, a
' button confirms.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

